     1	--- Page 1 ---
     2	Contents lists available at ScienceDirect
     3	Information and Software Technology
     4	journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/infsof
     5	On the road to interactive LLM-based systematic mapping studies
     6	Kai Petersena,b,∗,Jan M. Gerkenb
     7	aBlekinge Institute of Technology, Valhallavägen 1, 37141 Karlskrona, Sweden
     8	bFlensburg University of Applied Sciences (FUAS), Kanzleistraße 91-93, 24943 Flensburg, Germany
     9	A R T I C L E I N F O
    10	Keywords:
    11	Systematic mapping studies
    12	Large language models
    13	GPTA B S T R A C T
    14	Context: The research volume is continuously increasing. Manual analysis of large topic scopes and continu-
    15	ously updating literature studies with the newest research results is effort intensive and, therefore, difficult to
    16	achieve.
    17	Objective: To discuss possibilities and next steps for using LLMs (e.g., GPT-4) in the mapping study process.
    18	Method: The research can be classified as a solution proposal. The solution was iteratively designed and
    19	discussed among the authors based on their experience with LLMs and literature reviews.
    20	Results: We propose strategies for the mapping process, outlining the use of agents and prompting strategies
    21	for each step.
    22	Conclusion: Given the potential of LLMs in literature studies, we should work on a holistic solutions for
    23	LLM-supported mapping studies.
    24	1. Introduction
    25	The mapping  study methodology  is frequently  used in the context  of
    26	software  engineering.  Two guidelines  for mapping  studies  (MSs) were
    27	proposed  and are, to date, cited more than 7000 times.1The purpose  of
    28	mapping  studies  is to classify  research  and observe  trends.  In contrast,
    29	systematic  literature  reviews  (SLRs)  focus on synthesizing  evidence.
    30	Given the purpose  of mapping  studies,  a more extensive  set of studies
    31	should  be targeted,  as otherwise,  it would  make sense to conduct  an
    32	SLR directly.
    33	This article  discusses  the possibilities  of using LLMs throughout  the
    34	mapping  process,  proposing  strategies  for using LLMs in each research
    35	step of MSs. There are various  motivations  for using LLMs in MSs: (1)
    36	An increased  number  of published  papers  to be analyzed;  (2) Conduct-
    37	ing mapping  studies  on a larger scope; (3) Getting  additional  research
    38	design  ideas by interacting  with the LLM; (4) Reduced  effort allows
    39	updating  mapping  studies  more regularly.  The solution  described  here
    40	is a direction  for researchers  in the software  community  to work on
    41	solutions  as a community,  as a reliable  and intensively  evaluated  solu-
    42	tion will likely result from a community  effort. This article  may serve
    43	as a starting  point for discussing  using LLMs in MSs, working  towards
    44	a holistic  solution,  and evaluating  the solution  in the SE context.
    45	We describe  the solution  and provide  references  to relevant  studies.
    46	Our solution  is inspired  by [1], where the authors  propose  a sys-
    47	tem where testers  interact  with a search-based  software  test system,
    48	∗Corresponding  author at: Flensburg  University  of Applied  Sciences  (FUAS),  Kanzleistraße  91-93, 24943 Flensburg,  Germany.
    49	E-mail addresses: kai.petersen@hs-flensburg.de ,kai.petersen@bth.se (K. Petersen) ,jan.gerken@hs-flensburg.de (J.M. Gerken) .
    50	1According  to Google  Scholar,  August  2024.observing  the output  and the tester providing  feedback  to the search-
    51	based system  based on candidate  solutions.  In our solution  proposal,  we
